From b5b1c8a950a78158a37c2cb690ad3b0a7a6b6e6d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sebastian Werner <swernerx@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 22:25:39 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] perf(renderer): write text escapes through a raw output
 cursor

The text escaper emitted its result one piece at a time: every clean run
and every replacement went through `push_run`, which reserved, re-read the
string's length and capacity from memory, and wrote the new length back.
A text node is short and a replacement is at most six bytes, so that
bookkeeping ran as often as the copies it guarded.

Introduce `escape::cursor::Cursor`, which reserves once per escaper call
and then keeps the write pointer and the spare byte count in locals for
the rest of the call, committing the length once at the end. The growth
path is cold and out of line; it commits the bytes written so far before
reserving, so the string's length never covers bytes that were not
written and an unwind leaves it valid, only shorter. Replacements move
from a `[&str; 256]` of fat pointers to a table of packed words that hold
the text and its length in one lane, so a replacement is a single
eight-byte store plus an advance instead of a load, a branch and a copy.
The wide run copies stay in an out-of-line helper, as before, to keep the
loop body small.

Output is unchanged. `escape/tests.rs` gains an oracle that shares no
table or copy path with the escaper, and drives it through outputs with
no spare capacity, with exactly the reserved amount, and with less than
the replacements need, plus a pseudorandom sweep over the whole byte
range.

