From ad93ff638d961c93079bdf818647202d33fe9133 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sebastian Werner <swernerx@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 22:30:17 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] perf(renderer): write URL escapes through the same output
 cursor
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

`write_url_segment` still appended piece by piece, and percent encoding
was the worst of it: a non-ASCII byte pushed its `%HH` one `char` at a
time, so a Unicode path ran three growth checks and three length
write-backs per source byte.

Move the segment escaper onto the cursor introduced for text escaping.
The scan is untouched — the eight-byte scalar walk after a hit stays, as
do the percent-encoded non-ASCII runs and the IPv6 authority split — only
the output mechanics change: one reserve per segment, a write pointer and
a spare count in locals, one length write-back at the end. Table
replacements come from packed words as in the text escaper, and `%HH` is
packed the same way from a sixteen-byte hex table, so each escape is a
single store. The free `push_run`/`push_run_long` helpers had no callers
left and are removed; their copy strategy and the finding that keeps the
wide copies out of line now live on the cursor's methods.

The outer `reserve` in `write_url_escaped_into` is gone too, since every
segment reserves its own length.

Output is unchanged. `url/tests.rs` now spells its oracle out instead of
reading the escaper's tables, and adds capacity-shape and pseudorandom
sweeps plus a decode check for both kinds of packed replacement.

